Anime-Lists — anime-list-master.xml, anime-movieset-list.xml
https://github.com/Anime-Lists/anime-lists

Contributes the AniDB-to-TVDB id mapping (the tvdbId on each node) and the
movie-set grouping hints reported during a build.

The project publishes no explicit licence. What is taken from it is bare
identifier pairs — numbers linking one public database to another — which we
treat as facts rather than protected expression. Anyone redistributing this
dataset should reach their own view on that.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Wikidata — https://www.wikidata.org
Dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0.

Contributes two things. The character and staff names in data/, resolved from
the entity named by each node's authored wikidataId: the Japanese label becomes
the original name, the English label the en translation. And the English title
of a series, from the work's P1476 ("title") claim in English, or its English
label where it carries no such claim.

A series has no id to join on, so the work is found by looking its native title
up as a Japanese Wikipedia article during `builder init`, and is kept only when
the entity says it is a kind of work. The resulting wikidataId is recorded on
the series in data/, and an authored one overrides it.

That is all it contributes. The cast structure — which character appears in
which series, and who voices them — is not extracted from Wikidata. Neither is
any series' native title or romanization. Both are authored by hand in
config/overrides/, with Wikidata used as a research aid. Wikidata is credited
for the names and titles it supplies, not for that structure.

CC0 imposes no conditions. Wikidata is credited here because saying where facts
came from is worth doing, not because it is required.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NOT USED: AniList

AniList's Terms of Service prohibit using its API as a backup or data storage
service, and prohibit mass collection, so no AniList content is fetched, stored
or served. The `anilistId` fields in this dataset are identifiers parsed out of
the cross-reference URLs published by anime-offline-database; they are join keys
into that ODbL source, not AniList data. Consumers may use them to fetch
descriptive content live from AniList at runtime under AniList's own terms.
